issue(id="0008" status="review")
  title Add validation boundaries and useful formatter error reporting
  user-story
    as-a user of puggers
    i-want useful errors when syntax is malformed or unsupported
    so-that I can trust the tool and quickly understand when it is preserving versus rejecting input
  premise.
    The current formatter leans toward preservation and idempotence, which is
    appropriate for bootstrapping, but it also means malformed or unsupported
    syntax can silently pass through without clear boundaries. As the syntax
    model grows, the tool should become more intentional about what it accepts,
    what it preserves opaquely, and what it reports as an error. This will also
    matter for a future CLI or npm-facing developer experience. Task `0009`
    now gives us a broad upstream case and anti-case register, so the next step
    here is not discovering malformed inputs but deciding which of those
    currently preserved or rewritten anti-cases should instead become explicit
    validation failures, and how that should surface to users.
  acceptance-criteria
    criterion Define the categories of syntax handling: fully supported, preserved but not normalized, and unsupported or invalid.
    criterion Add useful error messages for malformed indentation and clearly broken syntax once the relevant constructs are structured.
    criterion Preserve lossless fallback behavior where practical, but make that behavior explicit rather than accidental.
    criterion Add tests that cover both successful preservation and intentional failures.
  review-items
    item(status="covered") Task `0009` now provides a pinned upstream anti-case baseline and checked-in behavior register, so `0008` can focus on turning current preserve-or-rewrite behavior into intentional validation categories and user-facing errors.
    item(status="covered") Default formatter behavior now stays permissive for recoverable syntax damage and surfaces line-based warnings instead of failing formatting.
    item(status="covered") The first recovery slice quarantines malformed indentation, bare `include` or `extends`, and missing control-flow expressions without dropping surrounding valid structure.
    item(status="covered") The current diagnostics remain internal parser or formatter groundwork because `dprint` does not expose a per-file warning channel; future richer consumers can wrap the same report shape without changing the recovery semantics.
    item(status="covered") The next recovery tranche started with orphaned structural keywords: `else` and `default` now stay structurally modeled while emitting warnings for invalid sibling context or trailing payload.
    item(status="open") Review the newly inventoried warnings on positive corpus fixtures such as `each.else`, `case`, and `mixin.attrs` to decide which should remain intentional recoveries versus which indicate overly eager invalid-syntax heuristics.
